course: dictionary
lesson: 688
title: Ban: what is not allowed
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To ban something is to say officially that it is not allowed. A ban is the rule that stops it. Ban is a verb and a noun: a school can ban phones in class, and there can be a ban on phones in class.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. «Meanwhile Jake's mum bans sleepovers for a month because he was rude to his teacher.» The words for a month and because tell you this is a punishment with a reason. Ask: what would fit here? Not allowed fits, so bans means says sleepovers are not allowed.
explain: Ban works in many places: at home, in school, in sport and between countries. «A serious first doping offence can bring a ban of up to 4 years.» In sport, a player can be banned from games for breaking the rules. Countries also ban things that are harmful, like some chemicals.
explain: Look for the little phrases that often go with it: ban something, ban someone from doing something, and a ban on something. These patterns help you spot the word and use it correctly.
fact: To ban means to officially say something is not allowed.
fact: A ban is a rule that stops something being done.
fact: Ban is a verb and a noun: ban, bans, banned, banning.
fact: «Banned» is the past form: «Lily was banned from the football team» means she was not allowed to play for it.
fact: Phrases to remember: ban something, ban someone from doing something, a ban on something.
exercise: «Meanwhile Jake's mum bans sleepovers for a month because he was rude to his teacher.» What does bans mean here? | Lets Jake have sleepovers every week | Gives Jake a new bike | *Says sleepovers are not allowed | Plans a big party for Jake
exercise: «A serious first doping offence can bring a ban of up to 4 years.» In this sentence, a ban means? | A test that is easy to pass | A prize for winning a race | *A rule that stops a player from playing | A new kit for the team
exercise: In «The school has a new ban on junk food», what kind of word is ban? | A full stop | A verb | *A noun | An adjective
test: «Lily was banned from the football team for breaking the rules.» What does banned mean here? | Chosen as captain | Given a free kit | Asked to train harder | *Not allowed to play for the team
test: Which phrase shows someone is stopped from doing something? | band someone together | *ban someone from doing something | ban the bus | bang someone on the head
exam: Which word is closest in meaning to banned in «Smoking indoors is banned in many countries»? | *Forbidden | Encouraged | Ignored | Celebrated
